    "title": "Hypothetical Local LLM Inference: Beyond Raw Tokens/Second",
    "body": "This post discusses a hypothetical experiment exploring local Large Language Model (LLM) inference speeds. In this scenario, two hypothetical runtimes on the same machine achieved 18 and 24 tokens per second respectively, using a single prompt (F1, F2). While a higher tokens/second rate seems advantageous, it's crucial to understand what this metric doesn't capture. This hypothetical test did not measure critical factors like time to first token (TTFT) or the actual quality of the generated answers (F3). TTFT significantly impacts perceived responsiveness, especially for interactive use cases. Similarly, a faster generation rate is less meaningful if the output is nonsensical or irrelevant. Therefore, this limited hypothetical sample does not establish a universal winner (F4). Evaluating local LLM performance requires a broader set of metrics beyond just raw throughput.",
