    "title": "Hypothetical LLM Inference: Why 24 t/s Isn't Always Better Than 18 t/s",
    "body": "We're examining a hypothetical local LLM inference scenario to highlight potential performance nuances. The experiment involves a single prompt run on identical hardware, yielding two hypothetical results: 18 tokens per second (t/s) and 24 t/s (F1, F2). While the 24 t/s figure appears superior, this hypothetical data is incomplete. Crucially, the experiment did not quantify the time taken to generate the *first* token, nor did it assess the semantic quality or factual accuracy of the responses (F3). For many applications, especially chat interfaces, the initial delay (TTFT) is a major contributor to user experience. A model generating 18 t/s with a near-instant TTFT might feel faster than a 24 t/s model with a noticeable lag before any output appears. Furthermore, raw speed without quality is unproductive. This hypothetical comparison, therefore, does not declare a definitive winner (F4) and underscores the need for comprehensive benchmarking.",
